=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The BIND9 `named` DNS daemon on `srv1` (10.5.0.4), specifically the `options` block of `/etc/bind/named.conf`.

**Mechanism:** `named.conf` contains the directive `recursion no;`, which configures the server as a purely authoritative resolver for the local `jeremysitlab.com` zone only. When any office client sends a recursive query for an external hostname (e.g., `google.com`), named immediately returns `REFUSED` with the flag "recursion not available." Since browsers and all OS-level name resolution depend on DNS to resolve hostnames before opening TCP connections, every attempt to load a web page fails at the resolution step — producing the symptom of pages "timing out" even though the underlying L3 internet path is fully functional (direct-IP ping to simulated internet IPs on ispa succeeds with 0% loss, r1's MASQUERADE NAT rules are intact, and OSPF routing reaches r1 correctly).

**Localization:** `srv1` (10.5.0.4), process `named` (BIND), file `/etc/bind/named.conf` — the single line `recursion no;` in the global `options` block.

**Root cause:** The `recursion no` directive was added to srv1's named.conf, silently downgrading the campus DNS server from a recursive resolver (which office clients depend on to look up any external hostname) to an authoritative-only server that can only answer for its own zone.

**Fix:** On `srv1`, change `recursion no;` to `recursion yes;` in `/etc/bind/named.conf` (and optionally add `allow-recursion { any; };` plus appropriate forwarders), then reload named: `rndc reload` or `systemctl reload bind9`.
=== END ===